2. DESIGN FOR AN AMERICAN HIGH SCHOOL STUDENT

Use language and interaction patterns understandable to a typical American high school student.

Assume the user:

* Has never used a CMS.
* Does not know what JSON means.
* Does not understand schemas.
* Does not know what a draft overlay is.
* May not understand course-track identifiers.
* Has never published educational content.
* May use the application from an iPhone.
* Wants to accomplish tasks without reading extensive documentation.

The user should be able to answer:

“What course am I editing?”

“What lesson am I editing?”

“How do I change something?”

“How do I save?”

“How do I preview?”

“Will students see my changes?”

“How do I publish?”

“How do I undo a mistake?”

The interface must answer these questions naturally.

Use plain, conversational American English.

Avoid unnecessary technical vocabulary.

Prefer short labels and straightforward explanations.

Do not use childish visuals or patronizing language.

The result should feel professional, modern, calm, and easy to understand.

