12. CLEAR DRAFT AND PUBLISHED STATES

The current Content Studio uses an isolated draft overlay to protect published content.

PRESERVE THIS IMPORTANT ARCHITECTURE.

However, explain the distinction in simple language.

Use these visible concepts:

PUBLISHED:
“What students currently see.”

DRAFT:
“Changes you’re still working on.”

UNPUBLISHED CHANGES:
“Edits you have saved but haven’t published.”

Display status clearly.

Examples:

“Published — No unpublished changes”

“Draft — 3 unpublished changes”

“Ready for Review”

“Published Successfully”

These labels must reflect actual system state.

Do not claim changes are saved when they are only in memory.

Do not claim publication succeeded unless the appropriate storage or publishing operation completed successfully.

The CMS should always make clear whether editing affects:

* The current published course.
* A private draft.
* A preview only.

Never overwrite the existing Ukrainian curriculum accidentally.

